  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM EDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nA largely stagnant synoptic pattern will allow for mild and dry \nconditions to continue likely through the end of October. Upper\nair observations reveal a large upper level ridge building over\nthe central CONUS this morning; as it gradually continues to \nbuild, the ridge will shift eastward in the coming days. An \nOmega Block pattern is likely to setup with sinking air \nunderneath the ridge yielding lots of sun, mild temperatures, \nand light winds. With an expansive area of surface high pressure\nalso expected to anchor over the eastern US, this pattern will \nlikely remain through the weekend into early next week. Expect \nseveral days of rinse and repeat weather with highs in the upper\n60s to low 70s and lows in the low to mid 40s each day. \n\nVery few changes were made to the forecast today as a persistence \nforecast was largely utilized through early next week. No rainfall \nis expected over the next 7 days; It is starting to become evident \nthat while a cold front may move through around next Tuesday, there \nwill not be sufficient forcing and moisture available for any \nrainfall with it. Towards the middle of next week, temperature \ngenerally trend towards normal with highs around 60 degrees as \nthe aforementioned synoptic pattern breaks down. \n\nWith the stagnant pattern locked in place, clear skies, and light \nflow will likely yield some patchy dense fog overnight into early \nFriday morning. Best chances for fog will likely be northeast of \nFort Wayne, IN. Much like the past few nights, the best timeframe \nfor dense fog will be between 3 AM to 10 AM EDT Friday, with the \nlowest visibilities around daybreak. A SPS or Dense Fog Advisory may \nbe needed overnight depending on trends and where any fog\ndevelops.",
